## Exact Block09 operator lift

Let

```text
D = {+-e_1,+-e_2,+-e_3},       tau=1/24.
```

For the qubit at signed site `epsilon e_j`, write
`sigma_k^(epsilon e_j)` for its Pauli operator. For either sign
`delta=+-1`, define

\[
 E_{\delta e_i}={I_{64}\over12}
 +{\tau\over4}\sum_{j,\epsilon}
 \epsilon\left(\delta_{ij}-{1\over3}\right)
 \sigma_j^{(\epsilon e_j)} .                              \tag{1}
\]

The two signs on a fixed axis have equal effects. They are a legitimate
two-channel refinement with ancillary branch randomness; they do not extract
an additional sign coordinate from the input.

For `c in {+-1}^3`, define

\[
 E_c={I_{64}\over16}
 +{3\tau\over32}\sum_{j,\epsilon}\epsilon
 \sum_{k\ne j}c_jc_k\,\sigma_k^{(\epsilon e_j)} .          \tag{2}
\]

Expanding the trace-free Block09 tensor shows coefficientwise that

\[
 \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[
 E_b\bigotimes_{n\in D}{I+v_n\cdot\sigma\over2}
 \right]=p_b(v)                                           \tag{3}
\]

for every six-tuple of Bloch vectors and every one of the fourteen labels.
The runtime effects contain no H1/H2 label, target tensor, source lookup, or
winner table.

## Full spectra, positivity, and uniqueness

Within one effect, the six nonidentity Pauli terms act at different sites and
commute. For an axis effect, two local coefficient norms are `1/144` and four
are `1/288`; their sum is `1/36`. Hence

\[
 \lambda(E_{\rm axis})\subseteq
 \left[{1\over12}-{1\over36},{1\over12}+{1\over36}\right]
 =\left[{1\over18},{1\over9}\right].                       \tag{4}
\]

For a corner effect, each of the six local coefficient vectors has norm
`sqrt(2)/256`, giving

\[
 \lambda(E_{\rm corner})\subseteq
 \left[{8-3\sqrt2\over128},{8+3\sqrt2\over128}\right].      \tag{5}
\]

Both lower endpoints are strictly positive. The primary enumerates all
`14 x 64` simultaneous-sign sectors; the independent checker separately
finds nine axis levels and seven corner levels with multiplicity 64.

The constants sum as

\[
 6\left({1\over12}\right)+8\left({1\over16}\right)=1,
\]

and every Pauli coefficient cancels over the label set, so

\[
 \sum_bE_b=I_{64}.                                         \tag{6}
\]

Finally, four one-qubit density operators span
`span{I,sigma_x,sigma_y,sigma_z}`. Their sixfold products have rank
`4^6=4096` and span the full six-qubit operator algebra. Equality (3) on all
product densities therefore fixes the linear operator extension uniquely.
This is a tomographic uniqueness statement for the displayed law, not a
derivation of the Born rule or of empirical frequencies.

## Explicit square roots

For label `b`, write its six local coefficient vectors as `w_(b,n)`. In the
joint eigenbasis of their normalized Pauli axes,

\[
 \lambda_b(z)=a_b+\sum_{n=1}^6z_n\lVert w_{b,n}\rVert ,
 \qquad z\in\{+1,-1\}^6.                                  \tag{7}
\]

With

\[
 P_{b,n,z}={I+z\,\widehat w_{b,n}\cdot\sigma^{(n)}\over2},
\]

the positive square root is

\[
 \sqrt{E_b}=\sum_z\sqrt{\lambda_b(z)}
 \prod_{n=1}^6P_{b,n,z_n}.                                \tag{8}
\]

The strengthened primary constructs every term in (8), squares all sector
eigenvalues, and performs the exact Walsh inversion. The constant and six
linear coefficients reproduce (1)--(2), and all products of order two through
six vanish. Thus (8) is an explicit operator certificate rather than an
abstract dilation placeholder.

